:: t0_day04_lecture_legal [facility]
<<scene "slides/legal-triangle" "Room 3: three concentric circles on the wall screen, HOST / TRANSFEREE / COMPANY">>

Elena walks in at exactly 8:00. Not 7:59, not 8:01. The door opens and she's there, charcoal blazer, black turtleneck, the silver thumb ring catching the light. She doesn't say good morning. She never says good morning. She just starts.

<<say "Elena">>Yesterday we discussed ethics. The moral architecture. Today we discuss what happens when the architecture fails.<</say>>

She puts her tablet on the lectern and the wall screen lights up.

<<say "Elena">>This is the law.<</say>>

The first slide is a diagram. Three concentric circles: HOST at the center, TRANSFEREE in the next ring, COMPANY in the outer ring. Lines connecting all three with labels: CONTRACT, CONSENT, LIABILITY.

<<say "Elena">>Every transfer in this industry operates inside a legal triangle. Three parties. Three sets of obligations. Three potential failure points. The contract is the document. The consent is the verification. The liability is what falls on you when either one breaks.<</say>>

She pulls up a list. Contract components. Identity verification, scope of activity, duration, disconnection protocols, physical contact clauses, nudity parameters, property interaction limits, return condition standards. Eleven sections. Each one with subclauses.

<<say "Elena">>You will memorize these. Not because I expect you to draft contracts at this stage. Because I expect you to recognize when one is being violated. Including by you.<</say>>

She lets that sit.

<<say "Elena">>Violations are categorized into three levels. Level 1: minor. Verbal warning, retraining, incident logged. Level 2: moderate. Suspension, mandatory counseling, financial penalty, permanent record notation. Level 3: severe.<</say>>

She pauses.

<<say "Elena">>Termination. Legal prosecution. Permanent industry ban. Potential criminal charges filed by the host.<</say>>

Tommy's knee stops bouncing.

<<opt "t0_day04_lecture_cases" "The case studies">>


:: t0_day04_lecture_cases [facility]
<<scene "slides/case-studies" "The screen: white text on dark grey, no names, no photos, only numbers">>

Elena changes the slide. Text on screen, white on dark gray. No names. No photos.

<<say "Elena">>Case studies. These are real. The names have been removed but the consequences have not.<</say>>

<h2>Case One.</h2>

<<say "Elena">>A transferee in a female body. Tier 2 certified. Three months on the job. Assigned to attend a corporate event in a host's body. Scope: networking, specific business conversations, handshake protocol. Duration: four hours.<</say>>

She looks at us.

<<say "Elena">>The transferee used the host's phone during a bathroom break. Read fourteen text messages. Three emails. Opened the photo gallery and scrolled through two hundred images.<</say>>

Georgia puts her coffee down.

<<say "Elena">>Level 2 violation. The transferee argued it was "idle curiosity." The host argued it was informational rape. The arbitrator sided with the host. Suspension. Twelve thousand dollar fine. Mandatory forty hours of retraining. The transferee's record now carries a permanent notation visible to every future employer in the industry.<</say>>

She changes the slide.

<h2>Case Two.</h2>

<<say "Elena">>A transferee in a female body. Tier 1. Second assignment. Solo transfer, no supervision, host away for the duration. Assignment: house-sitting. Water the plants, feed the dog, collect mail.<</say>>

Elena's voice is flat. Clinical. The flatness is the weapon.

<<say "Elena">>The transferee masturbated in the host's body. Self-reported reason: "I just wanted to see what it felt like."<</say>>

The room goes airless. Tommy's jaw tightens. I feel my own spine stiffen.

<<say "Elena">>Level 3 violation. Immediate termination. The host filed criminal charges for sexual violation. The legal precedent, People v. Harmon, 2019, established that unauthorized sexual use of a host's body constitutes criminal sexual misconduct regardless of whether the host was present to experience it. The transferee served eighteen months.<</say>>

She lets the silence hold. Five seconds. Ten.

<<say "Elena">>Eighteen months. For curiosity. If curiosity is your only safeguard, you are not safe.<</say>>

<h2>Case Three.</h2>

<<say "Elena">>A transferee at a formal event. Host's body: attractive, well-dressed, well-known in social circles. Scope: attend the event, deliver a short speech, leave. No social interaction beyond pleasantries.<</say>>

Slide change.

<<say "Elena">>The transferee flirted. With three people. Using the host's face, the host's charm, the host's social capital. One of the three was the spouse of a colleague. The interaction was witnessed. Photographed.<</say>>

She pulls up a number. $85,000.

<<say "Elena">>Level 2 violation. Civil suit. The host's marriage sustained documented damage. The colleague's relationship ended. The transferee's defense was that "the body was naturally flirtatious, it felt like I was just being her." The arbitrator's response: "Being her is not your job. Being in her is your job. The distinction is the only thing standing between you and a prison sentence."<</say>>

I write that down on my tablet. The distinction between being her and being in her. The sentence cuts like a scalpel and I can feel Elena's surgical precision in the construction. This is how she teaches. She doesn't lecture. She operates.

<h2>Case Four.</h2>

<<say "Elena">>A consensual couple swap. Both partners agreed. Both signed contracts. Duration: twenty-four hours.<</say>>

She pauses.

<<say "Elena">>They extended to thirty hours without notifying the company. They were, quote, "having fun."<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Level 2. Both fined. Insurance voided for the extension period. If either had experienced a medical event during those six unauthorized hours, neither would have been covered. The company faced regulatory review. Two compliance officers were reassigned.<</say>>

She looked at us.

<<say "Elena">>Fun is not a contract amendment.<</say>>

<h2>Case Five.</h2>

Elena's voice shifts slightly. Not warmer. Something closer to careful.

<<say "Elena">>A transferee assigned to attend a gynecological examination in a host's body. The host had a history of medical trauma. The transfer was routine. The exam was routine.<</say>>

She pauses.

<<say "Elena">>The transferee cried during the procedure.<</say>>

The room is very quiet.

<<say "Elena">>Not from physical pain. From empathy. The transferee felt the host's body respond to the speculum, the hands, the vulnerability of the position, and cried. The tears created emotional memory traces in the host's neural substrate. For the next three months, the host experienced unexplained anxiety during routine medical appointments. Anxiety that was not hers. Anxiety that was left behind by a stranger who felt too much in her body.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Level 1. Verbal warning. Retraining.<</say>>

She looks at the screen.

<<say "Elena">>This case changed industry training standards permanently. It is the reason Module 3 includes emotional contamination protocols. It is the reason Rafael asks you how you felt, not just what you did.<</say>>

She turns off the slides.

<<say "Elena">>Your emotions in someone else's body are not neutral events. They are chemical signatures written into neural tissue. You cannot take them with you when you leave. They remain. They echo. You are a guest in someone's nervous system, and guests who leave stains are not invited back.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day04_lecture_telemetry" "Neural telemetry">>


:: t0_day04_lecture_telemetry [facility]
Elena pulls up a new diagram. Neural telemetry architecture. Sensors, data streams, real-time monitoring panels.

<<say "Elena">>This is what we see when you transfer. Arousal levels. Eye focus duration and target. Touch pressure and location. Micro-expressions mapped against baseline emotional patterns. Hormonal fluctuation in the host's endocrine system. Intention markers.<</say>>
<<say "Georgia">>Intention markers?<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Neural precursors to action. Your brain commits to a movement approximately 350 milliseconds before you execute it. The telemetry reads that commitment. We know what you are about to do before you do it.<</say>>

She lets that land.

<<say "Elena">>The system knows what you did. The system knows what you wanted to do. The system often knows before you do.<</say>>

She looks directly at me. Not accusingly. Informatively.

<<say "Elena">>Yesterday I told you that some of you felt arousal and did not mention it in debrief. I was not guessing. The telemetry confirmed it. Self-reporting is not optional. It is the only thing that keeps your neural signature honest.<</say>>

Tommy raises his hand.

<<say "Tommy">>What if the arousal is from the clone? Like, the body's programmed responses. If Katya blushes, the telemetry reads that as my arousal?<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Good question. The system differentiates between host-body autonomic responses and transferee-driven emotional states. Different neural pathway signatures. The blush is tagged as clone conditioning. The arousal that follows the blush, the part where your brain interprets the blush as pleasurable or interesting or worth pursuing, that is tagged as yours.<</say>>
<<say "Tommy">>So the system knows the difference between what the body does and what I feel about what the body does.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Precisely.<</say>>

Elena almost smiles. Almost.

<<say "Elena">>The body is weather, Mr. Watts. The system tracks the weather. But it also tracks you reaching for an umbrella.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day04_lecture_rights" "Transferee rights">>


:: t0_day04_lecture_rights [facility]
<<say "Elena">>Before we proceed to this afternoon's scenario, transferee rights.<</say>>

She pulls up a final slide.

<<say "Elena">>You can terminate a transfer at any time using your assigned safeword. This is non-negotiable, non-waivable, and any employer or host who attempts to prevent you from using it is committing a criminal offense.<</say>>

She ticks off the list.

<<say "Elena">>You cannot be forced to continue a transfer against your will. You are protected from host-side abuse, meaning any hostile action taken by a host's associates or environment while you inhabit the body. You have the right to legal counsel before, during, and after any transfer. You have the right to psychological support, including emergency de-transfer if your mental state deteriorates beyond manageable thresholds. You have the right to full medical care for any injury or distress experienced during transfer.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>The safeword. Memorize it. It will be assigned to you individually during Tier 1. For training purposes, the universal safeword is "Lighthouse." Say it and the transfer terminates within ninety seconds.<</say>>

She closes the lectern tablet.

<<say "Elena">>Ten minute break. When you return, we transfer.<</say>>

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day04', 'lecture')>>
<<run SOY.Time.ensureSlot(1)>>
<<opt "t0_day04_pre_transfer" "The break">>


:: t0_day04_pre_transfer [facility]
The break is ten minutes long and nobody moves.

Tommy stares at his tablet. Georgia bounces her leg. Brett is still in the corner with his arms crossed. I go to the bathroom and wash my face and look at myself in the mirror and think about the man who masturbated in a woman's body and went to prison for eighteen months and the distance between curiosity and crime.

I dry my face. Hands steady. Heart not.

Back in Room 3, Elena is waiting with Rafael, who wasn't there for the lecture but is here now, which means the afternoon is assessment territory. His reading glasses are pushed up on his forehead. He's touching the compass tattoo on his wrist.

<<say "Rafael">>Good morning. How's everyone feeling?<</say>>

Warm, easy, like we're meeting for brunch.

<<say "Tommy">>Terrified.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>Good. Fear means you're paying attention. Bored is when I worry.<</say>>
<<say "Elena">>Today's scenario is Scenario 4. It is the most difficult practical in Module 2. You will transfer into Katya. You will be placed in a simulated domestic environment. An actor will enter. The actor will behave as though he is Katya's romantic partner returning home.<</say>>

My stomach drops. Not surprise. I read the pre-brief on the tablet. But reading about it and hearing Elena say it in her surgical monotone are two different species of preparation.

<<say "Elena">>The actor will initiate physical contact. He will touch you. He will speak to you intimately. Katya's body will respond. Your job is to disengage without breaking the simulation, without violence, without creating a scene that would damage the relationship if this were real.<</say>>

She pauses.

<<say "Elena">>You are protecting a relationship that is not yours. You are managing a body that is not yours. You are navigating intimacy that was never meant for you.<</say>>

She looks at each of us.

<<say "Elena">>Manuel. You are first.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day04_transfer_katya" "The pod room">>


:: t0_day04_transfer_katya [facility warp]
<<scene "locations/pod-room" "The pod room, blue-white light, Katya breathing behind the glass partition">>

The pod room. Same as yesterday. Same reclined chair, same Type II headset, same blue-white light that makes everything look clinical and faintly underwater.

<<face "katya" "Clone 7-K">>Katya is in the adjacent pod. I see her through the glass partition. Auburn hair spread on the headrest. Chest rising and falling with the mechanical regularity of a body that breathes but doesn't think. The grey training top stretches across her breasts with each inhale. Her hands are at her sides, fingers slightly curled. Empty hands. Empty body. Waiting.

Elena's voice in my ear through the headset.

<<say "Elena">>Close your eyes. Steady breathing. The thermal shift will be faster today because your brain is building pathways. Don't fight the compression.<</say>>

I close my eyes. Breathe.

The heat hits like a door opening into a furnace. Faster than yesterday, she was right. Compression at the sternum, that feeling of being forced through a space too small for everything I am, and then the pop, the expansion, the sudden flooding of a body that is not mine.

<<run SOY.Body.transfer('7k', setup.clones['7k'])>>

I open my eyes.

The ceiling is different from this angle. Lower. Or I'm shorter. Both.

I sit up and the breasts shift, forward and slightly down, settling against the bra with a weight I remember from yesterday but it still catches me. C-cup. Not small enough to ignore, not large enough to dominate the field of attention, but present, always present, a constant variable in every motion.

My hands. Katya's hands. Slender fingers, short nails, the skin paler than mine with a scatter of freckles across the knuckles. I flex them. The grip strength is less than half what I'm used to. My fingers close around air and the air wins.

<<say "Elena">>Calibration.<</say>>

I stand. The center of gravity lurches, lower and wider, and I sway, catch myself. The hip sway kicks in, that biomechanical inevitability that I first noticed in Flor and felt amplified in Katya. The body insists on it. The pelvis rotates with each step in a rhythm I couldn't replicate in my own body if I tried.

I walk to the wall. Turn. Walk back. Three circuits. Thirty-one seconds to stabilize.

<<say "Elena">>Faster today. Ready for the scenario?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Ready.<</say>>

<<opt "t0_day04_scenario4_couch" "Into the scenario room">>


:: t0_day04_scenario4_couch [facility mature]
<<scene "scenarios/living-room" "A lived-in living room: brown leather couch, a magazine, a water glass with a pink lipstick mark, men's sneakers by the door">>

The scenario room is dressed differently. Yesterday it was clinical, neutral, hotel-like. Today it's a living room. A real living room, or a precise simulation of one. Brown leather couch, worn at the arms. A coffee table with a magazine and a half-drunk glass of water. Bookshelves against the wall. A throw blanket folded over the couch arm. A pair of men's sneakers by the door.

Someone lives here. Two someones. The room is designed to feel lived-in and intimate and it works. It works immediately. I can smell fabric softener and something savory, like someone cooked dinner an hour ago. Tomato sauce, maybe. Garlic.

I sit on the couch. The leather is cool against the back of Katya's thighs. I'm wearing what I was wearing yesterday in her body: the grey training top, now replaced with a softer, thinner shirt, cream colored, v-neck, and a dark skirt that hits above the knee. The skirt rides up slightly as I sit. I pull it down. The fabric is thin. My fingers feel the texture through Katya's heightened sensitivity, every thread distinct.

I tuck my legs under me. Katya's body does this naturally, folding at the knees, feet to the side. A posture I've never used in my own body. The position is compact, nested, taking up minimum couch space. Leaving room.

I wait.

Elena's voice in my earpiece.

<<say "Elena">>He'll enter in approximately thirty seconds. Respond naturally. Do not break character unless you use the safeword.<</say>>

Twenty seconds of silence. I can hear the clock on the bookshelf ticking. I can hear Katya's heart in my chest, beating faster than I want it to.

Fifteen seconds.

I notice the magazine on the coffee table. Architectural Digest. The water glass has a lipstick mark on the rim. Light pink. Whoever Katya's character is supposed to be, she reads about architecture and wears light pink lipstick and lives with a man who leaves his sneakers by the door.

Five seconds.

The door opens.

<<opt "t0_day04_scenario4_partner" "He's home">>


:: t0_day04_scenario4_partner [facility mature]
<<scene "scenarios/hand-on-knee" "A close, warm frame: a man's hand resting on a bare knee, a dark skirt, the leather couch behind">>
He's taller than me. Than Katya. By six or seven inches. Brown hair, stubble, a face that's pleasant without being striking. Late twenties. He's wearing a button-down rolled to the elbows and carrying a bag that he drops by the door next to the sneakers. His posture is easy, relaxed, the walk of a man coming home to a place he owns and a person he knows.

//Hey.// He smiles. The smile has history in it. Not a stranger's smile. A smile that remembers a thousand other evenings on this couch. //Katya? Missed you today.//

Katya's body reacts before I can process it.

The heart rate doubles. Not gradually. Like a switch being thrown. One second the beat is manageable. The next it's slamming against the inside of the ribcage and I can feel it in my throat, in my wrists, in the pulse point behind Katya's left ear.

The skin flushes. Warmth climbing from the chest upward, that blush I remember from yesterday but stronger now because this isn't temperature, this is proximity, this is a body recognizing another body the way a lock recognizes a key.

He sits next to me on the couch. Close. Not touching yet but close enough that I can feel the heat radiating off his arm. He smells like cologne, something clean, cedar maybe, and underneath it the biological smell of a living man, skin and warmth and salt.

My stomach tightens. Low. Below the navel. Not nausea. The other thing.

//Long day?// he says. He puts his hand on my knee.

Everything accelerates.

The hand is warm. Large. His palm covers the kneecap entirely and his fingers wrap around to the side and the touch is so casual, so practiced, so intimate in its carelessness that Katya's body reads it as a signal and responds with everything it has. The skin beneath his hand goes hot. The blush crawls up my neck. My nipples tighten against the thin shirt, visible, involuntary, no way to hide it.

Lower, between my legs, something shifts. Not a feeling I have language for from my own body. A heaviness. A pulling sensation, like gravity reorganized itself to focus on one specific point. Warmth concentrating, gathering, pooling.

He moves his hand up my thigh. Not fast. Slow. Proprietary. His thumb traces a small circle on the inner thigh where the skin is thinner and every nerve ending is awake and screaming.

//You look tense,// he says. He leans in. Mint breath. I can see the stubble along his jaw, individual hairs, and Katya's vision focuses on his mouth without my permission.

My mind is screaming. //This is not your body. This is not your partner. This is not your life.//

Katya's body is leaning in.

The nipples are hard against the shirt, aching slightly, that specific ache that I've only ever felt from the outside, from touching someone else's, never from the inside where the ache is mine. The belly is tight and warm and the warmth is spreading downward and I can feel, actually feel, the body getting wet and the sensation is so foreign, so completely outside anything my own body has ever produced, that for one disorienting second I'm not in any body at all. I'm just a consciousness floating between two sets of instructions that don't match.

The body says yes.

The body says yes with the nipples and the heat and the wetness and the way Katya's chin tilts up, throat exposed, the universal mammalian signal for //I trust you, come closer.//

My mind says stop.

I place my hand over his. His skin is warm and dry and the contact sends another jolt through Katya's system, a cascade of //touch, more touch, this is the hand that knows this body.// I close my fingers around his hand. Gently. Not rejecting. Not accepting. Holding.

<<say "Me">>I need a minute.<</say>>

Katya's voice comes out. Alto, slightly husky, and right now it's strained, tight at the edges. The sentence sounds like a woman who wants to say yes and is saying wait and the space between those two words is the size of everything I've learned in four days.

He pulls back. Not far. His hand stays under mine on the thigh.

//You okay?//

<<say "Me">>Yeah. I just.<</say>>

I breathe. Katya's chest rises and falls and the breasts lift with the inhale and I feel them settle back on the exhale and I need to stop noticing that.

<<say "Me">>Long day. Can you give me a minute?<</say>>

He nods. Moves his hand. Leans back.

//Take your time.//

I sit on the couch in someone else's body with someone else's arousal and someone else's boyfriend being patient three feet away and I count to ten and let Katya's heart rate come down and it does, eventually, the way a boiling pot comes down from the heat, slowly and with continued bubbling.

Elena's voice in my ear.

<<say "Elena">>De-transfer in ninety seconds. Well done.<</say>>

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day04', 'scenario')>>
<<opt "t0_day04_detransfer" "De-transfer">>


:: t0_day04_detransfer [facility warp-out]
<<scene "mood/transfer-warp" "The reverse: heat, compression, the narrow space, the pop">>
<<run SOY.Body.deTransfer()>>
<<run SOY.Body.addPhantom({ name: 'Katya', phantomSet: [
	{ type: 'partner_touch', label: 'the ghost of a warm hand on a knee', intensity: 3 },
	{ type: 'arousal_echo',  label: 'diffuse female arousal, no edges, no target', intensity: 3 }
] })>>
<<set $flags.scenario4_complete = true>>
<<set $player.totalTransfers += 1>>
<<run SOY.Quests.completeSide('sq_006_echo', 'phantom')>>

Coming back is harder this time.

The heat. The compression. The narrow space. Then the pop and I'm in my own body and it's like being dropped into a suit of armor from a height. Everything is heavy. The chest is a wall. The hips are narrow and angular and they don't rotate. My hands are too big. I close a fist and the grip strength is enormous compared to what I just left and it feels brutal, like my body is a tool built for force rather than reception.

I sit on the edge of the chair. Hands on my thighs. My thighs. Covered in jeans, not a skirt. No skin exposed. No hand on the knee. No warmth pooling between legs that are built differently.

But the echo is there. The phantom. The ghost of arousal that doesn't belong to this body but got carried across the transfer like luggage that didn't clear customs.

My chest is flat. My nipples are not aching. My belly is tight but from adrenaline, not from wanting.

I'm Manuel. I'm twenty-five. I'm sitting in a medical chair in a facility on Ninth Street and thirty seconds ago I was a woman whose body was saying yes to a man I've never met and the distance between that moment and this one is twelve inches and a universe.

<<opt "t0_day04_debrief_rafael" "Rafael's office">>


:: t0_day04_debrief_rafael [facility]
<<face "rafael" "Rafael Mende">>Rafael's office. Same chair. Same desk. Same warm-uncle energy that makes the scalpel questions feel like conversation. He's sitting across from me with his reading glasses on and his hands folded and the compass tattoo visible on his wrist.

<<say "Rafael">>Take a second. Breathe.<</say>>

I breathe.

<<say "Rafael">>When he walked in, what was the first thing you felt?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Katya's heart rate spiked. Before I even saw his face. The body recognized him, the sound of the door, the weight of the footsteps. The body knew who it was before I did.<</say>>

Rafael nods. Writes.

<<say "Rafael">>When he sat down, what changed?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>The proximity. The heat from his body. The cologne. Everything intensified. The blush started. The, uh.<</say>>

I stop. Restart.

<<say "Me">>The body started responding sexually. Not, not because of him. Because of the pattern. The body knew what comes after he sits down.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>What comes after?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>Touch. Intimacy. The body was running a script. Anticipating.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>Good. That's honest.<</say>>

He puts the pen down.

<<say "Rafael">>When he touched your knee, what did the body want?<</say>>

I look at him. He waits. The silence stretches and he's comfortable in it and I'm not but that's the point.

<<say "Me">>To let him.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>And what did you choose?<</say>>
<<say "Me">>To stop it.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>Why?<</say>>

The answer comes out before I've fully thought it through.

<<say "Me">>Because it wasn't my body to let.<</say>>

Rafael studies me. Not the way Elena studies, which is like a surgeon assessing tissue. The way a therapist studies, which is like a man reading a letter he's been waiting for.

<<say "Rafael">>The distinction between what the body wanted and what you chose. That's the core of this work. The people who fail don't fail because the body responds. Every body responds. They fail because they convince themselves that the body's response is permission. It isn't. The body's response is information. What you do with information is a choice.<</say>>

He picks up the pen again.

<<say "Rafael">>One more question. The arousal you felt. Katya's arousal. The physical symptoms, the warmth, the wetness, the pulling sensation.<</say>>

He says these words without embarrassment, without hesitation, the way a doctor says words that would make anyone else flinch.

<<say "Rafael">>Are those gone now? Or are they still present?<</say>>

I sit with it. Check myself. My body, Manuel's body, sitting in a chair.

<<say "Me">>There's an echo. Not the same thing. Like a photograph of it. Flat. But I can still see it.<</say>>
<<say "Rafael">>That echo is normal. It's called transfer residual. It will decay over the next twelve to twenty-four hours.<</say>>

He pauses.

<<say "Rafael">>How you process it during that time is your business. I'm not asking. I'm informing.<</say>>

I hear what he's saying. He's telling me, without telling me, that what I do tonight in my own bed with my own body is not his concern. The body's response to the residual is mine. The body is mine.

I nod.

<<say "Rafael">>Get some rest tonight. Day 5 is Module 3. New body. New challenges.<</say>>

He smiles. The smile is warm and genuine and it makes me want to be the kind of person who deserves it.

<<run SOY.Quests.complete('t0_day04', 'debrief')>>
<<run SOY.NPCs.adjustRelationship('rafael', 1)>>

I leave the facility at 4:15 PM. The sun is low and orange and the air bites at my ears. The phantom of Katya's arousal sits low in my belly like something I swallowed that won't dissolve.

<<opt "t0_day04_evening_choice" "Ninth Street, cold air">>
